{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792894642,"nonce":"RORXQPAV31asrzS0/QrVmDQjpIk=","title":"Gc.dumpMemory leaves mark bits that make a later collection free live objects without generations","message":"hl.Gc.dumpMemory marks the heap and left the marks standing, on the reasoning that a mark only retains a block and the next sweep clears it. A later mark phase stops at an object it finds marked, so whatever that object points to after the dump is never traced and is freed.\n\nReproducer (from the caribou session): a static Array with one element; hl.Gc.dumpMemory(); push 20000 small arrays; hl.Gc.major(); push 200000 junk arrays; read the 20000 back. ASH_GC_GEN=0: 20000 of 20000 lost. Default collector: intact, because a major clears marks first and the minors use the card barrier, also with allocation-triggered minors and no explicit major. generational() is false on every non-unix platform, so the hole is the default there.\n\nFix: without generations every mark is dead outside a collection, so the dump clears them; with generations marks are sticky, so the dump makes the next collection a major, which clears them first. The comment on gc_dump_memory said the opposite and is corrected.\n\nRegression test: crates/ash_cli/tests/gc_dump_marks.rs runs the reproducer (DumpMarks.hx / dump_marks.hl) both ways.","files":null}]}